# .trivyignore - suppressed vulnerability findings.
#
# INTENTIONALLY EMPTY.
#
# Every entry added here silences a real finding, so each one must carry:
#
#   1. The CVE ID.
#   2. WHY it is not exploitable in this application specifically
#      (not "it's only a dev dependency" - justify the actual attack path).
#   3. A review date. Suppressions expire; they are not permanent decisions.
#   4. Who approved it.
#
# Format:
#
#   # CVE-2024-XXXXX
#   # Reason:   Vulnerable code path is in the CLI entrypoint, which this
#   #           service never invokes. No fix available upstream as of 2026-09.
#   # Review:   2026-12-01
#   # Approved: @Jeck-bot
#   CVE-2024-XXXXX
#
# Note that in THIS format, rules 3 and 4 are enforced by convention only -
# nothing stops a suppression outliving its review date except someone noticing.
# If entries ever accumulate here, migrate to `.trivyignore.yaml`, where Trivy
# parses an `expired_at` field itself and the suppression stops applying on that
# date whether or not anyone remembered:
#
#   vulnerabilities:
#     - id: CVE-2024-XXXXX
#       statement: Vulnerable code path is never invoked by this service.
#       expired_at: 2026-12-01
#
# A policy the tool enforces beats a policy written in a comment.
#
# An empty ignore file is the goal state. If this file starts growing, that is
# a signal that the severity threshold in ci.yml is mistuned - fix the
# threshold, don't paper over findings one by one.
